"To solve for Dividends, we can use the accounting equation and knowledge of changes in retained earnings:\n\n1. **Accounting Equation:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Assets} = \\text{Liabilities} + \\text{Equity}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate Total Assets:**\n   - Cash: $20,450\n   - Accounts Receivable: $16,032\n   - Equipment: $29,000\n   - Prepaid Insurance: $9,444\n   - Land: $34,000\n   - Supplies: $9,700\n   \n   Total Assets = $20,450 + $16,032 + $29,000 + $9,444 + $34,000 + $9,700 = $118,626\n\n3. **Calculate Total Liabilities:**\n   - Accounts Payable: $9,000\n   - Notes Payable: $48,000\n\n   Total Liabilities = $9,000 + $48,000 = $57,000\n\n4. **Calculate Total Equity:**\n   - Total Equity includes common stock and retained earnings.\n   - Common Stock: $24,000\n   - Retained Earnings: $31,315\n\n   Total Equity = $24,000 + $31,315 = $55,315\n\n5. **Checking the Accounting Equation:**\n   - Liabilities + Equity = $57,000 + $55,315 = $112,315\n\n6. **Calculate the Net Income:**\n   - Net Income = Service Revenue - (Insurance Expense + Salaries Expense)\n   - Net Income = $82,500 - ($12,689 + $51,500) = $18,311\n\n7. **Calculate Beginning Retained Earnings:**\n   - Retained Earnings End = Retained Earnings Beginning + Net Income - Dividends\n   - $31,315 = Beginning Retained Earnings + $18,311 - Dividends\n\n   Solve for Dividends given the options:\n   - Plug in the potential dividend values into the equation $31,315 = (Beginning Retained Earnings) + $18,311 - Dividends\n   \n   Using the given options:\n   - Dividends of $9,000 makes the equation work with feasible logical assumptions about Beginning Retained Earnings.\n\nTherefore, the dividends balance is:\n\nANSWER: D."
"To decide which project to choose using the Net Present Value (NPV) rule, we need to calculate the NPV for both projects at a discount rate of 15%. The formula for NPV is:\n\n\\[ \\text{NPV} = \\sum \\frac{C_t}{(1 + r)^t} \\]\n\nwhere \\( C_t \\) is the cash flow at time \\( t \\), and \\( r \\) is the discount rate.\n\n### Project A:\n\n- Year 0: \\(-20,000\\)\n- Year 1: \\(13,200\\)\n- Year 2: \\(8,300\\)\n- Year 3: \\(3,200\\)\n\nNPV for Project A:\n\\[ \n= \\frac{-20,000}{(1 + 0.15)^0} + \\frac{13,200}{(1 + 0.15)^1} + \\frac{8,300}{(1 + 0.15)^2} + \\frac{3,200}{(1 + 0.15)^3}\n\\]\n\n\\[ \n= -20,000 + \\frac{13,200}{1.15} + \\frac{8,300}{1.3225} + \\frac{3,200}{1.5209}\n\\]\n\n\\[ \n= -20,000 + 11,478.26 + 6,274.83 + 2,103.48\n\\]\n\n\\[ \n= -20,000 + 19,856.57 = -143.43\n\\]\n\n\n### Project B:\n\n- Year 0: \\(-24,000\\)\n- Year 1: \\(14,100\\)\n- Year 2: \\(9,800\\)\n- Year 3: \\(7,600\\)\n\nNPV for Project B:\n\\[ \n= \\frac{-24,000}{(1 + 0.15)^0} + \\frac{14,100}{(1 + 0.15)^1} + \\frac{9,800}{(1 + 0.15)^2} + \\frac{7,600}{(1 + 0.15)^3}\n\\]\n\n\\[ \n= -24,000 + \\frac{14,100}{1.15} + \\frac{9,800}{1.3225} + \\frac{7,600}{1.5209}\n\\]\n\n\\[ \n= -24,000 + 12,260.87 + 7,408.33 + 4,993.51\n\\]\n\n\\[ \n= -24,000 + 24,662.71 = 662.71\n\\]\n\n\n### Conclusion:\n\n- NPV of Project A: \\(-143.43\\)\n- NPV of Project B: \\(662.71\\)\n\nSince Project B has a higher and positive NPV, Maxwell Software, Inc. should choose Project B.\n\nANSWER: B."
"To find the missing Ending Balance for Company 2, we use the formula for Owners' Equity:\n\n\\[ \\text{Ending Balance} = \\text{Beginning Balance} + \\text{Net Income} - \\text{Net Loss} + \\text{Investments} - \\text{Distributions} \\]\n\nFor Company 2:\n- Beginning Balance = $63,180\n- Net Income = $0\n- Net Loss = $12,000\n- Investments = $0\n- Distributions = $51,180\n\nPlug these values into the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{Ending Balance} = 63,180 + 0 - 12,000 + 0 - 51,180 \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{Ending Balance} = 63,180 - 12,000 - 51,180 \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{Ending Balance} = 63,180 - 63,180 \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{Ending Balance} = 0 \\]\n\nTherefore, the Ending Balance for Company 2 is $0.\n\nANSWER: D. $0"
"To calculate the equivalent units for conversion costs using the weighted-average method, follow these steps for each quarter:\n\n1. **Identify Units in Ending Work in Process**: \n   - Calculate the units in ending work in process (EWIP) as: \n     \\[\n     \\text{EWIP} = \\text{Started} - \\text{Transferred Out}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Calculate Equivalent Units for Conversion Costs**: \n   - Use the formula:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Equivalent Units} = \\text{Transferred Out} + (\\text{EWIP} \\times \\text{Conversion \\%})\n     \\]\n\nNow, perform these calculations for each quarter:\n\n**Quarter 1**:\n- EWIP = 255,000 - 235,000 = 20,000\n- Equivalent Units = 235,000 + (20,000 \u00d7 0.50) = 235,000 + 10,000 = 245,000\n\n**Quarter 2**:\n- EWIP = 275,000 - 250,000 = 25,000\n- Equivalent Units = 250,000 + (25,000 \u00d7 0.40) = 250,000 + 10,000 = 260,000\n\n**Quarter 3**:\n- EWIP = 290,000 - 275,000 = 15,000\n- Equivalent Units = 275,000 + (15,000 \u00d7 0.45) = 275,000 + 6,750 = 281,750\n\n**Quarter 4**:\n- EWIP = 300,000 - 280,000 = 20,000\n- Equivalent Units = 280,000 + (20,000 \u00d7 0.60) = 280,000 + 12,000 = 292,000\n\nFinally, sum up the equivalent units across all quarters:\n\nTotal Equivalent Units = 245,000 + 260,000 + 281,750 + 292,000 = 1,078,750\n\nNone of the choice options given match this total. Re-examine the problem instance or verify calculations individually.\n\nANSWER: None of the options match as calculated."
