"To determine if this pedigree shows the pattern of the transmission of colorblindness, we should analyze if it is consistent with X-linked recessive inheritance, which is the typical mode for colorblindness.\n\n1. **Identify gender and affected individuals**: \n   - Males are squares and females are circles.\n   - Afflicted individuals are filled in solid black. Carriers are half-filled.\n\n2. **X-linked Recessive Characteristics**:\n   - More males are generally affected than females.\n   - Affected males have mothers who are carriers or affected.\n   - Daughters of affected males are carriers.\n   - Sons of affected males do not inherit the trait.\n\n3. **Analysis of the Pedigree**:\n   - **Generation I**:\n     - Female 1 is a carrier, as indicated by the half-filled circle.\n     - Male 2 is normal, given the open square.\n   - **Generation II**:\n     - Male 3 is afflicted, consistent with receiving the X-linked recessive allele from carrier female 1.\n     - Female 4 is a carrier, having received the normal X from her father and the affected X from her mother.\n     - Female 5 is normal, presumably receiving normal alleles.\n     - Male 6 is afflicted, receiving a carrier allele from Female 1.\n   - **Generation III**:\n     - Male 7 is normal, which is expected because his father (6) is afflicted, and his mother (5) might be a non-carrier.\n     - Female 8 is a carrier because her affected father (6) must pass down the affected X, but she appears normal.\n     - Male 9 is normal, receives one X from his mother (8), who is a carrier, and a normal X from his father.\n\n4. **Conclusion**: The pattern fits an X-linked recessive transmission. More males are affected, and females are carriers unless they are homozygous for the trait. Every affected male can be traced back to a carrier mother, which aligns with this pattern.\n\nANSWER: A."
