"The retinoblastoma protein (pRB) regulates the cell cycle by inhibiting the transcription factor E2F. When pRB is phosphorylated and inactive, E2F is free to promote the expression of genes required for the G1 to S phase transition.\n\nE2F target genes are involved in preparing the cell for DNA synthesis, which is a critical part of the S phase. These target genes typically encode proteins necessary for:\n\n- DNA replication, such as DNA polymerases.\n- DNA synthesis factors.\n- Other proteins related to S phase progression.\n\nGiven the options provided and the role of E2F in initiating the S phase, the proteins encoded by the target genes of E2F are most likely involved in DNA replication.\n\nANSWER: A."
