"To determine how the form of DNA will change upon digestion with HindIII, consider the following:\n\n1. **Form of the Plasmid**: Plasmids isolated from bacteria are typically in a supercoiled form, which is tightly coiled and compact.\n\n2. **Role of HindIII**: HindIII is a restriction enzyme that cuts DNA at specific recognition sites. Cutting the plasmid with HindIII will introduce a double-strand break at its specific site, thereby converting the supercoiled plasmid into a linear form.\n\n3. **Outcome**: When a supercoiled plasmid is cut once with a restriction enzyme like HindIII, it becomes linear, as it opens up the circular DNA into a straight line.\n\nBased on this reasoning, the form changes from supercoiled to linear.\n\nANSWER: E. Supercoiled to linear."
