"To find the enantiomer of the provided compound, we need to identify the stereochemistry of the given molecule and find the option with the opposite configuration.\n\nThe original compound (Image 1) shows:\n- The top stereocenter (with H and Br) has Br as the group with higher priority, and H as the lowest.\n- The bottom stereocenter (with Me and OH) has OH as the group with higher priority, and Me as the lowest.\n\nFor each stereocenter, reverse the configurations:\n- Top center: If the original compound is R, the enantiomer will be S (or vice versa).\n- Bottom center: If the original compound is R, the enantiomer will be S (or vice versa).\n\nNow let\u2019s look at the options:\n\nA. Image 2: Reverse both configurations of the original compound.\nB. Image 3: Holds same configurations as the original compound.\nC. Image 4: Reverse one configuration of the original compound.\nD. Image 5: Reverse both configurations of the original compound.\n\nBy visualizing and/or using the CIP rules, we can determine:\n- A and D match option for being enantiomers because they both reverse the configurations of the original compound.\n\nSince these are the same as enantiomers, the correct option should be one of them. Given the position order, consider that only one correct answer is allowed following a specified order, ANSWER: A."
"To find the mass of calcium hydroxide dissolved in Solution A, we can use the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{mass} = \\text{moles} \\times \\text{molar mass} \\]\n\nFirst, calculate the number of moles of calcium hydroxide in Solution A using its concentration and volume:\n\n1. Convert the volume from milliliters to liters:\n   \\[ 500 \\, \\text{mL} = 0.500 \\, \\text{L} \\]\n\n2. Use the molarity definition to find moles:\n   \\[ \\text{moles} = \\text{molarity} \\times \\text{volume} = 0.350 \\, \\text{M} \\times 0.500 \\, \\text{L} = 0.175 \\, \\text{mol} \\]\n\n3. Calculate the mass using the molar mass:\n   \\[ \\text{mass} = 0.175 \\, \\text{mol} \\times 74.093 \\, \\text{g/mol} = 12.966275 \\, \\text{g} \\]\n\nTherefore, the mass of calcium hydroxide dissolved in Solution A is approximately 12.97 g.\n\nANSWER: C"
"To determine the number of chiral centers in morphine (excluding nitrogen centers), we need to identify the sp\u00b3 hybridized carbon atoms that have four different substituents.\n\n1. **Identify sp\u00b3 Hybridized Carbons:**\n   - These carbons are shown with single bonds.\n\n2. **Count Chiral Centers:**\n   - Examine each sp\u00b3 hybridized carbon in the structure.\n   - Determine if it has four different groups.\n\nBy analyzing the structure of morphine in the image:\n\n- **Chiral Center 1:** The carbon attached to the hydroxyl group on the left.\n- **Chiral Center 2:** The carbon at the junction of the epoxy bridge (connecting the two rings).\n- **Chiral Center 3:** The carbon adjacent to the oxygen in the central ring.\n- **Chiral Center 4:** The carbon attached to the hydroxyl group on the left side in the bottom ring.\n- **Chiral Center 5:** The carbon connected to the methyl group and nitrogen.\n\nThere are **five chiral centers** in total.\n\nANSWER: B. 5."
"To determine the predominant species at point E on the titration curve, we consider the reaction involved and the equivalence point.\n\nThe reaction of sodium oxalate (\\(Na_2C_2O_4\\)) with hydrochloric acid (\\(HCl\\)) can be represented in two stages:\n1. \\(Na_2C_2O_4 \\rightarrow 2Na^+ + C_2O_4^{2-}\\)\n2. \\(C_2O_4^{2-} + H^+ \\rightarrow HC_2O_4^-\\)\n\nSince point E is the first equivalence point, the predominant species will be those resulting from the neutralization of half the \\(C_2O_4^{2-}\\) ions to \\(HC_2O_4^-\\).\n\nTherefore, at E the solution primarily contains \\(Na^+\\), \\(Cl^-\\), and \\(HC_2O_4^-\\).\n\nANSWER: B"
