"To determine which cellular changes are occurring within the myocardium, consider the following:\n\n1. **Context**: The patient suffered a cardiac arrest, and after 40 minutes of resuscitation, a thrombolytic agent was administered. This situation suggests a myocardial infarction (heart attack) with subsequent reperfusion injury.\n\n2. **Reperfusion Injury**: When blood flow is restored to the previously ischemic myocardium, free radicals are generated. These free radicals can cause cellular damage.\n\n3. **Analysis of Options**:\n   - **A. Apoptosis**: Programmed cell death is not the immediate response expected after reperfusion in the context of acute myocardial ischemia and reperfusion injury.\n   - **B. Free radical injury**: This is most likely to occur due to the oxidative stress associated with reperfusion of the ischemic myocardium.\n   - **C. Heterophagocytosis**: Involves phagocytosis of external particles and is unlikely in the context of immediate cellular response post-reperfusion.\n   - **D. Squamous metaplasia**: This is a form of cellular adaptation more common in epithelial cells subjected to chronic irritation or transformation and is irrelevant in this acute cardiac context.\n\nANSWER: B. Free radical injury."
