"To determine the correct answer, let's analyze the scenario:\n\n1. **Clinical Presentation**: \n   - The second pregnancy of this woman shows minimal fetal movement and a diagnosis of hydrops fetalis at 19 weeks.\n   - The baby is born at 32 weeks with marked icterus (jaundice) and severe anemia (Hct of 18%), indicating hemolytic disease of the newborn.\n\n2. **History**:\n   - The first pregnancy was uncomplicated, resulting in a healthy baby girl. \n   - The issues arise in the second pregnancy.\n\n3. **Pathophysiology**:\n   - The combination of hydrops fetalis, severe anemia, and marked jaundice points towards a hemolytic condition, likely due to an immunological reaction.\n   - The most common immunological cause in such scenarios, especially when the first pregnancy was normal, is Rh incompatibility. In Rh incompatibility, maternal antibodies target fetal red blood cells causing hemolysis.\n\n4. **Immunologic Mechanism**:\n   - The death of fetal red blood cells is mediated by maternal IgG antibodies crossing the placenta and attaching to the Rh antigen (D antigen) on fetal red blood cells.\n   - This results in complement-mediated hemolysis of fetal red blood cells.\n\n5. **Microscopic Appearance**:\n   - While the specific microscopic appearance isn't detailed here, in Rh disease the placenta can exhibit signs consistent with increased red blood cell destruction.\n   \nGiven these considerations, the likely immunologic mechanism involves complement-mediated cell destruction due to maternal anti-D antibodies affecting the fetal red cells.\n\nANSWER: C. Complement mediated cell destruction."
