"To answer the question, we first need to determine the underlying pathological process in the scenario described.\n\n1. The patient is experiencing chest pain for 4 hours with ST-segment elevation on the EKG and elevated cardiac troponin levels. These findings are indicative of an acute myocardial infarction (MI), commonly known as a heart attack.\n\n2. In an MI, the primary pathological process occurring in the heart muscle (myocardium) is ischemic injury due to lack of blood supply. This leads to cellular death and necrosis.\n\n3. The type of necrosis typically seen in myocardial infarction is coagulative necrosis, where the architecture of the tissue remains but the cells are dead. This is a characteristic response to ischemic injury in most solid organs except the brain.\n\nConsidering these points, the most likely finding related to the gross appearance of the myocardial infarction depicted in the image is coagulative necrosis.\n\nANSWER: C. Coagulative necrosis."
