"To find \\( J(0) \\), we need to calculate the cost function when \\(\\theta_1 = 0\\). The hypothesis becomes \\( h_\\theta(x) = 0 \\).\n\nFrom the plot, we can determine the \\(y\\) values for each \\(x\\):\n\n- For \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\)\n- For \\(x=2\\), \\(y=2\\)\n- For \\(x=3\\), \\(y=3\\)\n\nSubstitute these into the cost function:\n\n\\[\nJ(0) = \\frac{1}{2m} \\sum_{i=1}^m (h_\\theta(x^i) - y^i)^2\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= \\frac{1}{2 \\times 3} \\left((0 - 1)^2 + (0 - 2)^2 + (0 - 3)^2\\right)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= \\frac{1}{6} \\left(1 + 4 + 9\\right)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= \\frac{1}{6} \\times 14\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= \\frac{14}{6}\n\\]\n\nThus, \\( J(0) = \\frac{14}{6} \\).\n\nANSWER: D"
