"To determine which condition the abnormal fiber seen with Gomori trichrome staining is typical of, we should consider the staining patterns associated with each disorder:\n\n- **Hypoxia**: This condition generally does not show a distinct staining pattern on Gomori trichrome stain.\n\n- **A mitochondrial disorder**: On Gomori trichrome staining, mitochondrial disorders can present as \"ragged red fibers.\" These fibers stain red due to the abnormal accumulation of mitochondria beneath the sarcolemma.\n\n- **Polymyositis**: This condition typically presents with inflammatory infiltrates, and the muscle fibers do not have the specific staining pattern seen in the image.\n\n- **Nemaline myopathy**: This disorder is characterized by the presence of \"rod bodies\" or nemaline bodies within the muscle fibers, which may not be distinctly red with Gomori trichrome stain.\n\nThe image shows fibers with a reddish hue, consistent with \"ragged red fibers,\" which are typical of a mitochondrial disorder.\n\nANSWER: B."
