"To find \\( v_x \\), we need to apply nodal analysis at the node with voltage \\( v_x \\). Let's consider the components connected to this node and write the equation based on Kirchhoff\u2019s Current Law (KCL), which states that the sum of currents flowing into the node equals the sum of currents flowing out.\n\nComponents connected to the node:\n- The current source of 1 A flows directly out of the node.\n- A 4 A current source directs current into the node.\n- A 20 \u03a9 resistor connects \\( v_x \\) to the reference node.\n\nThe controlled sources depend on voltages at other nodes, which we need to express in terms of \\( v_x \\).\n\nAt the node corresponding to \\( v_x \\), the currents are:\n\n1. Current through the 20 \u03a9 resistor: \\(\\frac{v_x}{20}\\).\n2. Total current entering from the 4 A source.\n3. Total current leaving is also from the 1 A source.\n\nSetting up the KCL equation at the node:\n\n\\[ 4 A - 1 A - \\frac{v_x}{20} = 0 \\]\n\n\\[ 3 = \\frac{v_x}{20} \\]\n\nSolving for \\( v_x \\):\n\n\\[ v_x = 3 \\times 20 = 60 \\, \\text{V} \\]\n\nTherefore, the numerical value of \\( v_x \\) is 60 V.\n\nANSWER: A."
"To find the collector-to-emitter voltage, \\( V_{CE} \\), we need to analyze the circuit. The transistor is in the common-emitter configuration, and we'll assume it's in active mode.\n\n1. **Base Current (\\( I_B \\)):**\n\n   \\[\n   V_{CC} = I_B R_B + V_{BE}\n   \\]\n\n   Assuming \\( V_{BE} \\approx 0.7 \\, \\text{V} \\) for silicon transistors, we can solve for \\( I_B \\):\n\n   \\[\n   I_B = \\frac{V_{CC} - V_{BE}}{R_B} = \\frac{24 \\, \\text{V} - 0.7 \\, \\text{V}}{960 \\, \\text{k}\\Omega}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   I_B = \\frac{23.3}{960 \\times 10^3} = 24.27 \\times 10^{-6} \\, \\text{A} = 24.27 \\, \\mu\\text{A}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Collector Current (\\( I_C \\)):**\n\n   \\[\n   I_C = \\beta I_B = 80 \\times 24.27 \\, \\mu\\text{A} = 1.94 \\, \\text{mA}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Voltage across \\( R_C \\) (\\( V_{RC} \\)):**\n\n   \\[\n   V_{RC} = I_C R_C = 1.94 \\, \\text{mA} \\times 7 \\, \\text{k}\\Omega = 13.58 \\, \\text{V}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Collector-to-Emitter Voltage (\\( V_{CE} \\)):**\n\n   \\[\n   V_{CE} = V_{CC} - V_{RC}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   V_{CE} = 24 \\, \\text{V} - 13.58 \\, \\text{V} = 10.42 \\, \\text{V}\n   \\]\n\nBased on the calculations, we determine the value of \\( V_{CE} \\) is 10.42 V. The closest letter choice reflecting 10.42 V will provide the answer. If choices were provided, align the numerical solution with the appropriate letter.\n\nANSWER: B"
"To find the Laplace transform of the given periodic square waveform, we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a periodic function:\n\n\\[ F(s) = \\frac{1}{1 - e^{-sT}} \\int_{0}^{T} e^{-st} f(t) \\, dt \\]\n\nFor the given waveform:\n- The waveform has a period \\( T \\).\n- The function \\( f(t) = 1 \\) for \\( 0 \\leq t < T/2 \\) and \\( f(t) = 0 \\) for \\( T/2 \\leq t < T \\).\n\nWe compute the integral from \\( 0 \\) to \\( T \\):\n\n\\[ \\int_{0}^{T} e^{-st} f(t) \\, dt = \\int_{0}^{T/2} e^{-st} \\cdot 1 \\, dt + \\int_{T/2}^{T} e^{-st} \\cdot 0 \\, dt \\]\n\n\\[ = \\int_{0}^{T/2} e^{-st} \\, dt = \\left[-\\frac{1}{s} e^{-st}\\right]_{0}^{T/2} \\]\n\n\\[ = -\\frac{1}{s} (e^{-s(T/2)} - e^{-0}) = \\frac{1}{s} (1 - e^{-s(T/2)}) \\]\n\nNow, substitute this back into the Laplace transform formula:\n\n\\[ F(s) = \\frac{1}{1 - e^{-sT}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{s} (1 - e^{-s(T/2)}) \\]\n\nThus, the Laplace transform is:\n\n\\[ F(s) = \\frac{1 - e^{-s(T/2)}}{s(1 - e^{-sT})} \\]\n\nComparing this result to the given options, the correct answer is:\n\nANSWER: A."
