"To find the pore water pressure (\\( u \\)) at point G in the earth dam, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Determine the Effective Head at Point G:**\n\n   - The storage height (total head at upstream) is 16m.\n   - The total head decreases across the dam according to the flow network through equipotential lines.\n   - Since there are 22 equipotential lines, the potential drop per interval (\u2206h) is:\n     \\[\n     \\Delta h = \\frac{16 \\, \\text{m}}{22} = \\frac{8}{11} \\, \\text{m}\n     \\]\n   - At point G on the 20th line from downstream, it has passed through 2 intervals (22 - 20 = 2 potential drops):\n     \\[\n     \\text{Head at point G} = 16 \\, \\text{m} - 2 \\times \\frac{8}{11} \\, \\text{m} = 16 \\, \\text{m} - \\frac{16}{11} \\, \\text{m} = \\frac{160}{11} - \\frac{16}{11} = \\frac{144}{11} \\, \\text{m} \\approx 13.09 \\, \\text{m}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the Pore Water Pressure (\\( u \\)):**\n\n   - The pore water pressure at point G depends on the water head above it.\n   - Point G is 11.5m above the ground level. Therefore, the total height of head influencing G:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Pore water pressure} = \\gamma_w \\times \\text{Height} \n     \\]\n   - Using the density of water (\\( \\gamma_w = 9.81 \\, \\text{kN/m}^3 \\)), the Absolute head at G:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Height to point G = Head at point G - height above ground = } 13.09 \\, \\text{m} - 11.5 \\, \\text{m} = 1.59 \\, \\text{m}\n     \\]\n   - Then the pore water pressure is: \n     \\[\n     u = 9.81 \\, \\text{kN/m}^3 \\times 1.59 \\, \\text{m} \\approx 15.61 \\, \\text{kPa}\n     \\]\n\nThe value obtained (15.61 kPa) is closest to the option A: 30 kPa. \n\nTherefore, the closest answer to the options provided is:\nANSWER: A."
