"To determine which type of water is located on the surfaces of individual soil particles and is largely unavailable to plants, we must examine the properties of each type of water listed:\n\nA. Gravitational water is excess water that moves through the soil due to gravity and is available only temporarily until it drains away. It is not held strongly by the soil particles and evaporates readily or drains into the ground.\n\nB. Capillary water is water held around soil grains by capillary tension. This water is available to plants as it is not held as tightly and can be taken up by plant roots.\n\nC. Hygroscopic water is water held tightly to the surface of soil particles by molecular attraction and adhesion. This type of water is not available to plants because it is held too firmly and does not evaporate easily.\n\nGiven the description that the water is on the surfaces of individual soil particles and is unavailable to plants due to strong molecular attractions, the correct answer is:\n\nANSWER: C. Hygroscopic water."
