"To determine the reason why Japan's early attempts at introducing the Chinese-style imperial exam were largely unsuccessful:\n\nA. The Japanese were unable to adapt to the Chinese style of examination.\n   - While adaptation issues could be a challenge, it's unlikely the core reason, as Japan often adapted other Chinese systems.\n\nB. Merchants resisted, fearing that efficient administrators would lead to increased taxes.\n   - Merchants' resistance due to tax concerns is possible but doesn't align with historical accounts of why the system wasn't implemented.\n\nC. Japanese nobles feared that their power base would be weakened.\n   - The power in Japan was traditionally held by the nobility and the imperial family, and implementing a merit-based system could undermine their hereditary privileges and power structures. This fear of weakening their power base is historically a significant reason for resistance.\n\nD. Christian missionaries discouraged the adoption of pagan practices.\n   - Christianity was not introduced in Japan until the mid-16th century, well after early attempts to adopt the exam system, making this reason historically inaccurate for this period.\n\nBased on historical context, the option that aligns most closely with factual evidence is C.\n\nANSWER: C."
