"First, let's identify and define the terms given in the options:\n\nA. Resolution: This is the part of the story where the main conflict is resolved, and the story concludes. It is typically the end or the last event of the narrative.\n\nB. Exposition: This is the introductory part of a story that provides background information and sets up the scene, characters, and primary conflict. It occurs at the beginning, not the end.\n\nC. Conflict: This is the central struggle or problem that drives the plot. It occurs throughout the story, not specifically at the end.\n\nD. Rising Action: This is the series of events that lead up to the climax of the story. It builds tension and develops the conflict, occurring before the climax and resolution.\n\nNow, referring to the image, which states \"And they lived happily ever after,\" this phrase commonly signifies the conclusion of a story in which all conflicts are resolved. Therefore, the term that matches the end of the story or the very last event is \"Resolution.\"\n\nANSWER: A. Resolution."
"To determine which term best describes the practice of creating and sharing a sketch after reading a book to reflect on its significance or connections, we examine the options:\n\nA. Fact Book Stretch: This option doesn't specify creating sketches related to story reflection, and \"Fact Book\" suggests focus on factual content rather than reflection of significance.\n\nB. Sketch to Stretch: This option directly implies sketching to interpret or extend the understanding of a book's story, reflecting on its significance.\n\nC. Science Fiction Stretch: This suggests a focus on science fiction content rather than creating reflective sketches for any book.\n\nD. Informational Picture Book: This typically refers to a factual picture book and doesn't relate to sketching as a reflective practice.\n\nGiven these descriptions, the best match for the practice described is B. Sketch to Stretch.\n\nANSWER: B"
