"To find the average normal stress (\\(\\sigma\\)) developed on the cross-section, we use the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\sigma = \\frac{P}{A}\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\(P\\) is the axial force.\n- \\(A\\) is the cross-sectional area of the material resisting the load.\n\n1. **Calculate the cross-sectional area \\(A\\):**\n\n   The cross section is annular (a ring), so the area \\(A\\) is:\n\n   \\[\n   A = \\pi (r_{\\text{outer}}^2 - r_{\\text{inner}}^2)\n   \\]\n\n   where:\n   - \\(r_{\\text{outer}}\\) is the outer radius = 100 mm = 0.1 m\n   - \\(r_{\\text{inner}}\\) is the inner radius = 80 mm = 0.08 m\n\n   \\[\n   A = \\pi ((0.1)^2 - (0.08)^2) \\, \\text{m}^2\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   A = \\pi (0.01 - 0.0064) \\, \\text{m}^2\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   A = \\pi (0.0036) \\, \\text{m}^2\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   A \\approx 0.01131 \\, \\text{m}^2\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate normal stress \\(\\sigma\\):**\n\n   Given \\(P = 300 \\, \\text{kN} = 300,000 \\, \\text{N}\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\sigma = \\frac{300,000}{0.01131} \\, \\text{Pa}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\sigma \\approx 26,529,715 \\, \\text{Pa} = 26.53 \\, \\text{MPa}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Conclusion:**\n\n   The average normal stress developed is approximately 26.53 MPa.\n\n   The normal stress distribution over the cross-section is uniform because the load is axial and the cross-section is symmetric.\n\nANSWER: A."
