"To calculate the thermal stress \\(\\sigma_{T}\\) in the beam, we'll use the formula for thermal stress in a constrained beam:\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{T} = - E \\alpha \\Delta T\n\\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\(E = 29,000 \\text{ ksi}\\) is the modulus of elasticity.\n- \\(\\alpha = 6.5 \\times 10^{-6}/^\\circ\\text{F}\\) is the coefficient of thermal expansion.\n- \\(\\Delta T = 20^\\circ\\text{F}\\) is the temperature change.\n\nNow let's substitute these values into the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{T} = - (29,000 \\text{ ksi}) \\times (6.5 \\times 10^{-6}/^\\circ\\text{F}) \\times (20^\\circ\\text{F})\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{T} = - 29,000 \\times 6.5 \\times 10^{-6} \\times 20\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{T} = - 29,000 \\times 0.0000065 \\times 20\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{T} = - 3.77 \\text{ ksi}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the thermal stress in the beam is \\(-3.77 \\text{ ksi}\\).\n\nANSWER: B."
