"The chemical structure in the image is cisplatin (cis-diamminedichloroplatinum(II)), which is a chemotherapy drug. Let's evaluate each statement:\n\nA. The chlorine groups are displaced before the drug becomes active.\n   - True. Cisplatin becomes active when the chloride ligands are replaced by water molecules in the cell, leaving a reactive aquated species that can bind DNA.\n\nB. Intrastrand cross-linking of DNA results from the action of the agent.\n   - True. Cisplatin primarily forms intrastrand cross-links between adjacent guanine bases in DNA, leading to bending and unwinding of the DNA helix.\n\nC. Base pairing between adenine and thymine is disrupted by the agent.\n   - Untrue. Cisplatin primarily forms cross-links between guanine bases, affecting guanine-cytosine base pairs rather than adenine-thymine base pairs.\n\nD. The compound acts as a metallating agent.\n   - True. Cisplatin is considered a metallating agent because it introduces platinum into the DNA structure, altering its conformation.\n\nThe untrue statement is C.\n\nANSWER: C."
