"To determine the IUPAC name of the compound in the image, we need to analyze its structure:\n\n1. **Identify the Functional Group:** The compound contains a carbonyl group (C=O) in the middle, which indicates that it is a ketone.\n\n2. **Number the Carbon Chain:** Start numbering from the end of the chain closest to the carbonyl group to give the ketone the lowest possible number. The chain consists of five carbon atoms, so it is a pentane derivative.\n\n3. **Determine the Position of the Carbonyl Group:** The carbonyl group is on the third carbon atom when numbered correctly (counting from either end due to symmetry, it is still on the third carbon).\n\n4. **IUPAC Naming for Ketones:** The suffix for a ketone is \"-one,\" and the position of the carbonyl group is indicated by a number. Therefore, the compound is 3-pentanone.\n\nGiven the choices:\n- A. cyclopentane: This would be a cyclic structure, not matching the image.\n- B. 2-methylpropan-2-ol: This would be an alcohol, which is not present in the structure.\n- C. 3-pentanone: Matches the structure identified.\n\nANSWER: C. 3-pentanone."
"To solve the questions, we will first extract the necessary information from the Nutrition Facts label:\n\n**a. Conversion Factor (calories per gram of fat):**\n\n- Calories from Fat = 15\n- Total Fat = 2g\n\nConversion factor = \\( \\frac{\\text{Calories from Fat}}{\\text{Total Fat}} = \\frac{15 \\text{ Calories}}{2 \\text{ g}} = 7.5 \\text{ Calories/g} \\)\n\n**b. Mass for 20 servings in kilograms:**\n\n- Each serving size = 30g\n- Total mass for 20 servings = \\( 20 \\times 30 \\text{ g} = 600 \\text{ g} = 0.6 \\text{ kg} \\)\n\n**c. Mass of protein in micrograms:**\n\n- Protein per serving = 2g\n- Convert grams to micrograms: \\( 2 \\text{ g} = 2,000,000 \\text{ \u03bcg} \\)\n\n**d. Significant figures for calories per gram:**\n\n- The input data from the label is provided as whole numbers (15 and 2), implying one significant figure.\n- Hence, the result should have 1 significant figure.\n\n**ANSWER: D**"
"To determine the direction of the total magnetic force on wire P, we can use the right-hand rule and the principle that parallel currents attract and anti-parallel currents repel.\n\n1. **Wire Layout and Current Direction**:\n   - All currents are coming out of the page.\n   - Wire P is on the right.\n\n2. **Force between Parallel Wires**:\n   - The force on a current-carrying wire due to another wire with parallel current will be attractive.\n   - The force on a current-carrying wire due to another wire with anti-parallel current will be repulsive.\n\n3. **Forces on Wire P**:\n   - The wire directly to the left of P exerts an attractive force towards the left.\n   - The wire directly below P exerts an attractive force upwards.\n   - The wire diagonally to the left and down from P exerts an attractive force towards it, affecting both the left and upward directions.\n\n4. **Resultant Force Calculation**:\n   - The horizontal attractive forces from the wires on the left will add up and result in a net force to the left.\n   - The vertical components will cancel each other out because they have equal magnitude and opposite direction from the bottom wire and the diagonal wire.\n\nTherefore, the net force on wire P is directed to the left.\n\nANSWER: A."
"To solve this, let's apply Lenz's Law, which states that the induced current will flow in a direction that opposes the change in magnetic flux. \n\n1. **Initial Situation:** The loop is initially around the north pole of the magnet. As the loop is pulled away, the magnetic field through the loop decreases.\n\n2. **Induced Current Direction:** To oppose the decrease in the magnetic field, the induced current will produce a magnetic field in the same direction as the original field, which is from the north to the south of the magnet. \n\n3. **Direction of Induced Magnetic Field:** Using the right-hand rule, the induced magnetic field inside the loop will be towards the left, opposing the reduction of the magnetic field from the north pole.\n\n4. **Force on the Magnet:** According to Newton's third law, if the loop exerts a magnetic force to the left to oppose the magnet's field reduction, the magnet will feel a force to the right due to the reaction.\n\nTherefore, the correct choice is:\nANSWER: A. To the right/To the right."
"To determine the direction of the force between two parallel wires carrying currents, we can use Amp\u00e8re's force law. When two parallel wires carry current in the same direction, they experience an attractive force towards each other. When they carry currents in opposite directions, they repel each other.\n\nGiven:\n- Both wires carry current \\( i \\) into the page, so the currents are in the same direction.\n\nTherefore, the force acting on the right-hand wire due to the current in the left-hand wire is attractive, meaning it is directed toward the left-hand wire.\n\nANSWER: B."
"To determine the speed of light in the plastic, we can use Snell's law, which is given by:\n\n\\[ n_1 \\sin \\theta_1 = n_2 \\sin \\theta_2 \\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\( n_1 \\) and \\( n_2 \\) are the refractive indices of air and plastic, respectively.\n- \\( \\theta_1 \\) is the angle of incidence.\n- \\( \\theta_2 \\) is the angle of refraction.\n\nIn this case:\n- The angle of incidence \\( \\theta_1 \\) = 37\u00b0\n- The angle of refraction \\( \\theta_2 \\) = 53\u00b0\n- Refractive index of air, \\( n_1 \\) \u2248 1.00.\n\nRearranging Snell's law to solve for \\( n_2 \\):\n\n\\[ n_2 = \\frac{n_1 \\sin \\theta_1}{\\sin \\theta_2} \\]\n\nNow substitute the values:\n\n\\[ n_2 = \\frac{1.00 \\cdot \\sin(37\u00b0)}{\\sin(53\u00b0)} \\]\n\nCalculate \\( \\sin(37\u00b0) \\) and \\( \\sin(53\u00b0) \\):\n\n- \\( \\sin(37\u00b0) \\approx 0.6018 \\)\n- \\( \\sin(53\u00b0) \\approx 0.7986 \\)\n\n\\[ n_2 = \\frac{0.6018}{0.7986} \\approx 0.7539 \\]\n\nThe speed of light \\( v \\) in plastic can be related to the refractive index:\n\n\\[ v = \\frac{c}{n_2} \\]\n\nWhere \\( c \\) is the speed of light in a vacuum (\\( c = 3 \\times 10^8 \\) m/s):\n\n\\[ v = \\frac{3 \\times 10^8}{0.7539} \\approx 3.98 \\times 10^8 \\, \\text{m/s} \\]\n\nNone of the options directly match this calculation. Therefore, let's recalculate for any possible typographical error. \n\nRe-evaluate on calculation, rounding may cause differences.\n\nUsing the closest rounded value calculation to identify option:\n\n\\[ v \\approx \\text{Option closest to calculation must be accepted or revisited.} \\]\n\nNo direct match implies recalibration required or interpretation of question.\n\nConcluding:\n\nANSWER: C. 1.13\n\n[Value finding needs reconciliation or approximate best value.]"
"To find the average speed between 3 and 4 seconds, we use the formula for average speed:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Average speed} = \\frac{\\text{Distance traveled}}{\\text{Time interval}}\n\\]\n\n1. **Determine the positions at 3 and 4 seconds:**\n   - Position at 3 seconds = 9 cm\n   - Position at 4 seconds = 16 cm\n\n2. **Calculate the distance traveled between 3 and 4 seconds:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Distance} = 16 \\, \\text{cm} - 9 \\, \\text{cm} = 7 \\, \\text{cm}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the time interval:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Time interval} = 4 \\, \\text{s} - 3 \\, \\text{s} = 1 \\, \\text{s}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the average speed:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Average speed} = \\frac{7 \\, \\text{cm}}{1 \\, \\text{s}} = 7 \\, \\text{cm/s}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the average speed of the ball between 3 and 4 seconds is 7.0 cm/s.\n\nANSWER: B"
"To solve this problem, we can use Newton's Third Law of Motion, which states that for every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction. This means that the force exerted by the tractor on the trailer is equal in magnitude and opposite in direction to the force exerted by the trailer on the tractor.\n\nNo matter the mass of the trailer compared to the tractor, the forces are equal in magnitude but opposite in direction.\n\nTherefore, the force that the trailer applies on the tractor is equal to the force of the tractor on the trailer.\n\nANSWER: C."
"To solve the problem, let's examine each option:\n\nA. The eccentricity of the orbit is less than zero.\n- Eccentricity of an orbit cannot be less than zero. It ranges from 0 (for a circle) to 1 (for a parabolic trajectory).\n\nB. The eccentricity of the orbit is greater than 1.\n- Eccentricity greater than 1 represents a hyperbola, not an elliptical orbit.\n\nC. The Sun might be at point C.\n- In an elliptical orbit, the Sun is located at one of the foci. Point C is further from the major axis center, suggesting it could be a focus.\n\nD. The Sun might be at point D.\n- Point D is along the major axis and less likely to be a focus based on the diagram symmetry.\n\nE. The Sun might be at point B.\n- Point B is near the minor axis, unlikely to be at a focus.\n\nGiven the definition of elliptical orbits:\n- The focus (where the Sun is) is located along the major axis of the ellipse.\n- Only option C (point C) appropriately suggests the position along the major axis where the Sun might be located in such diagrams.\n\nANSWER: C"
"To determine the torque produced by a force, the formula is:\n\n\\[\n\\tau = r \\cdot F \\cdot \\sin(\\phi)\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\(\\tau\\) is the torque,\n- \\(r\\) is the distance from the pivot,\n- \\(F\\) is the magnitude of the force,\n- \\(\\phi\\) is the angle between the force and the rod.\n\nGiven that the force \\(F\\) is initially applied at an angle \\(\\theta\\) to the rod, the effective perpendicular component of the force is \\(F \\cdot \\sin(\\theta)\\). The torque produced in this initial configuration is:\n\n\\[\n\\tau = L \\cdot F \\cdot \\sin(\\theta)\n\\]\n\nTo produce the same torque when the force is applied perpendicular to the rod (\\(\\phi = 90^\\circ\\)) at some distance \\(r\\), the torque equation simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\tau = r \\cdot F\n\\]\n\nSetting the two expressions for torque equal, we get:\n\n\\[\nL \\cdot F \\cdot \\sin(\\theta) = r \\cdot F\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(r\\):\n\n\\[\nr = L \\cdot \\sin(\\theta)\n\\]\n\nThus, the force should be applied at a distance \\(L \\cdot \\sin(\\theta)\\) from the axis to produce the same torque.\n\nANSWER: A"
"To solve for the force exerted by the rope, we need to consider the torques about different points. The torques about a point are generated by the force the rope exerts and the weight of the rod. To find the torque, we need to choose a point where other forces (like normal reaction and friction) are not contributing to the moment because they would complicate the calculation.\n\n1. **Point 1**: This is the point where the rope is tied to the wall. Taking torques about this point eliminates the force of tension as a contributing torque directly, which is not helpful in finding that force.\n\n2. **Point 2**: Taking torques about this point (where the rod is in contact with the wall), we can find an expression for the tension in the rope by setting it equal to the torque due to the weight of the rod. This is because the normal force at point 2 does not exert torque about point 2.\n\n3. **Point 3**: This is the center of mass of the rod, and considering torques about this point can include both the tension and the wall force, which complicates isolating the tension.\n\n4. **Point 4**: If torques are taken about point 4, the force at the wall complicates the expressions for torque, as we are unable to ignore the forces there.\n\nGiven these considerations, the best choice to solve for the tension in a straightforward manner involves option 2, where the torque calculation directly gives the force from the rope since the normal force at point 2 does not contribute.\n\nANSWER: B."
"To solve this, consider the behavior of each system when it undergoes simple harmonic motion:\n\n1. **Spring-Mass System (m1):**\n   - **Equilibrium Position:** The spring is neither compressed nor extended, hence the net force is zero.\n   - **Kinetic Energy:** Is maximum at the equilibrium position because the speed is greatest.\n   - **Potential Energy:** The spring potential energy is lowest (zero) at the equilibrium position.\n\n2. **Pendulum (m2):**\n   - **Equilibrium Position:** The pendulum is at its lowest point, directly under the pivot point.\n   - **Kinetic Energy:** Is maximum at the equilibrium position due to maximum speed.\n   - **Potential Energy:** The gravitational potential energy is lowest at the equilibrium position because height is minimum.\n\nGiven this information:\n\n- For **both spheres**, the kinetic energy is maximum as they pass through their equilibrium positions.\n\nThus, the correct answer is:\n\nANSWER: A. The maximum kinetic energy is attained as the sphere passes through its equilibrium position."
"To determine the minimum thickness of the glass that results in constructive interference for orange light, we can use the concept of thin film interference. \n\n### Steps:\n\n1. **Understand the Problem**: We have a thin film of glass (n = 1.50) surrounded by air (n = 1.00) and water (n = 1.33). The light ray is incident normally onto the glass.\n\n2. **Condition for Constructive Interference**:\n   - Constructive interference occurs when the path difference between the reflecting waves is an integral multiple of the wavelength.\n   - The wavelength inside the glass is given by \\(\\lambda_{\\text{glass}} = \\frac{\\lambda_{\\text{air}}}{n_{\\text{glass}}}\\).\n\n3. **Calculate Wavelength in Glass**:\n   - \\(\\lambda_{\\text{air}} = 600\\, \\text{nm}\\)\n   - \\(\\lambda_{\\text{glass}} = \\frac{600\\, \\text{nm}}{1.50} = 400\\, \\text{nm}\\)\n\n4. **Determine Thickness for Minimum Path Difference**:\n   - The minimum thickness \\(t\\) for constructive interference is such that:\n   \\[\n   2t = m\\lambda_{\\text{glass}}\n   \\]\n   where \\(m\\) is the order of interference. For the first (minimum) constructive interference, \\(m = 1\\).\n\n5. **Calculate Minimum Thickness**:\n   \\[\n   2t = 1 \\times 400\\, \\text{nm} \\implies t = \\frac{400}{2} = 200\\, \\text{nm}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the minimum thickness that results in constructive interference and an orange color is 200 nm.\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nANSWER: D"
"To determine the charge on the electroscope leaves in each step, let's consider the process:\n\nI. **Negatively Charged Rod Near (Leaves Spring Apart):**\n- A negatively charged rod is brought near the electroscope, inducing a separation of charges. The electrons in the electroscope move away from the rod, causing the leaves, which become negatively charged, to repel each other.\n\nII. **Touched with Finger (Leaves Collapse):**\n- By touching the electroscope with a finger, it gets grounded, allowing electrons to flow away from the electroscope to the ground, neutralizing the negative charge. As a result, the leaves collapse because the excess charge is removed.\n\nIII. **Finger and Rod Removed (Leaves Spring Apart Again):**\n- When the finger is removed first, then the rod is removed, the electroscope remains with a deficiency of electrons (since electrons flowed out to the ground), leaving it positively charged. The leaves repel each other due to like charges.\n\nTherefore, in scenario I, the charge separation is due to induction, and the presence of a negative charge on the leaves from the accumulated electrons near the leaves makes them repel. In scenario III, after the grounding and removal of the negative rod, the remaining positive charge causes the leaves to repel.\n\nANSWER: D. negative in I, positive in III."
"To determine when the object passes through its initial position again, we need to analyze the velocity-time graph and compute the displacement of the object. The displacement is given by the area under the velocity-time curve.\n\n1. From \\( t = 0 \\) to \\( t = 1 \\) second, the velocity is negative (approximately -1 m/s). The area of this triangle is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 1 \\, \\text{s} \\times (-1 \\, \\text{m/s}) = -0.5 \\, \\text{m}\n   \\]\n\n2. From \\( t = 1 \\) to \\( t = 2 \\) seconds, the velocity is positive and increases to 2 m/s linearly. The area of this trapezoid is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times (1 \\, \\text{s} + 1 \\, \\text{s}) \\times (0 \\, \\text{m/s} + 2 \\, \\text{m/s}) = 1 \\, \\text{m}\n   \\]\n\n3. From \\( t = 2 \\) to \\( t = 3 \\) seconds, the velocity remains constant at 2 m/s. The area is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area} = 2 \\, \\text{m/s} \\times 1 \\, \\text{s} = 2 \\, \\text{m}\n   \\]\n\n4. From \\( t = 3 \\) to \\( t = 4 \\) seconds, the velocity decreases back to 0 m/s. The area of this trapezoid is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times (1 \\, \\text{s} + 1 \\, \\text{s}) \\times (2 \\, \\text{m/s} + 0 \\, \\text{m/s}) = 2 \\, \\text{m}\n   \\]\n\nThe total displacement from \\( t = 0 \\) to \\( t = 2 \\) seconds is:\n\\[\n-0.5 \\, \\text{m} + 1 \\, \\text{m} = 0.5 \\, \\text{m}\n\\]\n\nTo return to the initial position, the object needs to accumulate a total area of \\(+0.5 \\, \\text{m}\\) to compensate for the initial \\(-0.5 \\, \\text{m}\\). After \\( t = 2 \\) seconds, at \\( t = 0.5 + 0.5 = 2.5 \\) seconds, the object achieves an area of \\(-0.5 \\, \\text{m} + 1 \\, \\text{m} + 1 \\, \\text{m} = 1.5 \\, \\text{m}\\). However, this would exceed placement, so the object exactly balances the total areas at:\na. Between 2 and 3 seconds.\n\nThus, the object passes through its initial position again:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{D} \n\\]\n\nANSWER: D."
"To determine the speed of light in each medium, we use Snell's Law, which relates the angle of incidence and refraction to the refractive indices. The refractive index \\( n \\) of a medium is inversely proportional to the speed of light in that medium. Therefore, if the light bends towards the normal, the medium has a higher refractive index (and light travels slower), and if it bends away, the refractive index is lower (light travels faster).\n\n1. **From \\( n_1 \\) to \\( n_2 \\)**:\n   - The light bends towards the normal, indicating that \\( n_2 > n_1 \\).\n   - Therefore, \\( v_1 > v_2 \\).\n\n2. **From \\( n_2 \\) to \\( n_3 \\)**:\n   - The light bends away from the normal, indicating that \\( n_3 < n_2 \\).\n   - Therefore, \\( v_3 > v_2 \\).\n\nCombining these observations:\n- From \\( v_1 > v_2 \\) and \\( v_3 > v_2 \\), we can infer \\( v_3 > v_1 > v_2 \\).\n\nThus, the correct answer choice is:\n\nANSWER: A."
"To find the thickness that results in maximum constructive interference, we need to consider the conditions for constructive interference in thin films. For normal incidence, constructive interference occurs when the optical path difference (OPD) between the two reflected beams gives a phase difference of an integer multiple of \\(2\\pi\\).\n\n1. **Phase change upon reflection:**\n   - When light reflects from a medium with a higher index of refraction, a half-wavelength phase shift (\\(\\pi\\)) occurs.\n   - In this case, light reflecting from the top surface of the film (\\(nf\\) to \\(n_1\\)) will not experience a phase shift because \\(nf > n_1\\).\n\n2. **Phase change for the second beam:**\n   - Light transmitting through the film reflects off the bottom surface (\\(nf\\) to \\(n_2\\)) and back through the film.\n\n3. **Consider the path difference:**\n   - The light travels down and back through the film, so the optical path length is \\(2t\\), where \\(t\\) is the thickness of the film.\n\n4. **Condition for constructive interference:**\n   \\[\n   2t = m\\lambda_{\\text{film}},\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\lambda_{\\text{film}} = \\frac{\\lambda_0}{n_f}\\) is the wavelength of light in the film, and \\(m\\) is an integer.\n\n5. **Account for phase changes:**\n   - The reflection from the top surface results in a half-wavelength phase shift, so the correct condition for constructive interference, including this shift, is:\n     \\[\n     2t = (m + \\frac{1}{2})\\lambda_{\\text{film}}.\n     \\]\n\nFor maximum constructive interference where \\(m=0\\):\n\\[\n2t = \\frac{1}{2}\\lambda_{\\text{film}} \\implies t = \\frac{1}{4}\\lambda_{\\text{film}}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\lambda_{\\text{film}} = \\lambda\\), we have:\n\\[\nt = \\frac{\\lambda}{4}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the correct film thickness for maximum constructive interference is \\(\\frac{\\lambda}{4}\\).\n\nANSWER: D."
"To determine the direction of the average velocity, we need to consider the displacement of the particle from point P to point Q. In circular motion, displacement is a vector pointing directly from the initial position to the final position.\n\n1. **Identify Initial and Final Positions**: \n   - The particle moves from Point P to Point Q.\n   - Point Q is exactly halfway around the circle from Point P.\n\n2. **Displacement Vector**:\n   - The displacement is a straight line from P to Q.\n   - This line is the diameter of the circle.\n\n3. **Direction of the Displacement**:\n   - From the diagram, the displacement is directly leftward since P is on the right and Q is on the left across the diameter.\n\n4. **Average Velocity**:\n   - The average velocity vector has the same direction as the displacement vector.\n\nThus, the average velocity during this time interval is leftward.\n\nANSWER: B. leftward."
"To identify the correct graph, consider the relationship given by Ohm\u2019s Law, which states:\n\n\\[ V = IR \\]\n\nFor power \\( P \\) dissipated in a resistor, the formula is:\n\n\\[ P = VI \\]\n\nSubstituting Ohm's Law into the power equation gives:\n\n\\[ P = I^2R \\]\n\nThis equation suggests that power \\( P \\) is proportional to the square of the current \\( I \\). Therefore, if a component obeys Ohm's Law, the graph showing how power \\( P \\) varies with current \\( I \\) is a quadratic curve.\n\nBy examining the graphs:\n\n- Graph A shows a curve that rises quadratically as \\( I \\) increases.\n- Graph B shows a linear decrease.\n- Graph C shows a linear increase.\n- Graph D shows a curve that decreases as \\( I \\) increases.\n\nThe correct representation of \\( P = I^2R \\) is a quadratic curve, which is Graph A.\n\nANSWER: A"
"To solve this problem, we need to understand the effect of closing the switch \\( S \\) on the circuit:\n\n1. **Initial Circuit (Switch Open):** Bulbs 1, 2, and 3 form a series circuit. Bulb 4 is not illuminated because the switch is open.\n\n2. **Effect of Closing the Switch:** \n   - By closing the switch \\( S \\), bulb 4 is added in parallel with bulb 3.\n   - In a parallel circuit, the voltage across each parallel branch is the same. This means bulbs 3 and 4 each now have the same voltage across them as bulb 2 does.\n\n3. **Changes in Brightness:**\n   - **Bulb 3:** With bulb 4 in parallel, the combined resistance of bulbs 3 and 4 is reduced compared to just bulb 3 alone. As a result, more current flows through that parallel section, reducing the current through bulb 2. The voltage across bulbs 3 and 4 remains the same because they are in parallel, so the brightness of bulb 3 remains unchanged.\n   - **Bulb 4:** It turns on since it's now part of the closed parallel path.\n   - **Bulb 2:** It becomes dimmer because the total circuit resistance decreases, allowing more current to flow through the branch containing bulbs 3 and 4.\n   - **Bulb 1:** Since the current through the branches is divided among the parallel section, the voltage remains the same across the entire series circuit, including bulb 1.\n\nGiven these points, the only bulb affected in terms of brightness (besides bulb 4 turning on) is bulb 2, which becomes dimmer. None of the other bulbs become brighter.\n\nANSWER: B. Bulb #2 only."
"To determine the correct graph showing the relationship between the maximum kinetic energy \\( K \\) of photoelectrons and the frequency of incident light, we apply the photoelectric effect equation:\n\n\\[ K = hf - \\phi \\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\( K \\) is the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons.\n- \\( h \\) is Planck's constant.\n- \\( f \\) is the frequency of the incident light.\n- \\( \\phi \\) is the work function of the material.\n\nFrom this equation, we see that \\( K \\) is linearly dependent on the frequency \\( f \\), with the slope \\( h \\) and an intercept \\(-\\phi\\) on the energy axis when \\( f \\) is zero. The graph should thus be a straight line but does not start from the origin due to the work function \\(\\phi\\).\n\nThus, the correct graph is a straight line with a positive slope and a negative intercept on the frequency axis.\n\nANSWER: A"
"To determine the work function (\u03d5) from the graph, we can use the photoelectric equation:\n\n\\[ E_{km} = hf - \\phi \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( E_{km} \\) is the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons,\n- \\( h \\) is Planck's constant,\n- \\( f \\) is the frequency of the incident photons,\n- \\( \\phi \\) is the work function of the material.\n\nThe work function corresponds to the frequency where the kinetic energy \\( E_{km} = 0 \\). This frequency is the intercept on the frequency axis (x-axis) where the line crosses.\n\nFrom the graph, the line appears to intercept the x-axis around \\( f = 4 \\times 10^{14} \\, \\text{Hz} \\).\n\nUsing the known value of Planck's constant in electron volts:\n\\[ h = 4.14 \\times 10^{-15} \\, \\text{eV}\\cdot\\text{s} \\]\n\nCalculate the work function:\n\\[ \\phi = h \\times f \\]\n\\[ \\phi = 4.14 \\times 10^{-15} \\, \\text{eV}\\cdot\\text{s} \\times 4 \\times 10^{14} \\, \\text{Hz} \\]\n\\[ \\phi = 1.656 \\, \\text{eV} \\]\n\nRound 1.656 eV to one of the given options, which is closest to:\n1.5 eV.\n\nANSWER: A."
"To determine which point shows the repulsive nature of the strong nuclear force, we need to look for where the force is positive, as repulsive forces push nucleons apart and are usually depicted in the positive region of a force vs. distance graph.\n\n1. The graph indicates the strong nuclear force as a function of nucleon separation (distance).\n2. Repulsive forces appear when the force value is positive.\n3. Point A is in the positive region of the force axis, indicating a repulsive force. \n\nANSWER: A"
"To solve this problem, consider the following steps:\n\n1. **Energy Levels in the Bohr Model**: In the hydrogen atom, energy levels are quantized. The energy difference between two levels (n=3 to n=2 or n=3 to n=1) determines the frequency of the emitted light.\n\n2. **Energy Difference Calculation**: The energy difference between two levels is greater when the transition is to a lower level. Therefore, the transition from n=3 to n=1 has a greater energy difference than from n=3 to n=2.\n\n3. **Frequency and Energy Relationship**: According to the Planck-Einstein relation, the frequency (\\(f\\)) of emitted light is directly proportional to the energy difference (\\(E\\)), \\(E \\propto f\\).\n\n4. **Wavelength and Frequency Relationship**: The wavelength (\\(\\lambda\\)) is inversely proportional to the frequency, \\(\\lambda \\propto \\frac{1}{f}\\).\n\n5. **Photon Speed**: The speed of light is constant in a vacuum and doesn\u2019t change with frequency or wavelength.\n\n6. **Photon Momentum**: The momentum (\\(p\\)) of a photon is \\(p = \\frac{E}{c}\\), where \\(c\\) is the speed of light. Greater energy implies greater momentum.\n\nApplying this reasoning to the options:\n- A transition from n=3 to n=1 results in higher energy and hence:\n  - Higher frequency.\n  - Shorter wavelength.\n  - Greater momentum.\n\nThus, in comparison to the n=3 to n=2 transition, the n=3 to n=1 transition will emit light with greater momentum.\n\nANSWER: D."
"To determine the magnitude and direction of the current induced in the loop, we can use Faraday's Law of Electromagnetic Induction and Ohm's Law.\n\n**Faraday's Law:**  \nThe induced electromotive force (emf) in a loop is given by the rate of change of magnetic flux through the loop:\n\n\\[\n\\text{emf} = -\\frac{d\\Phi_B}{dt}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\Phi_B = B \\cdot A\\) is the magnetic flux, \\(B\\) is the magnetic field, and \\(A\\) is the area of the loop.\n\n**Given:**\n- The side of the square loop \\(s = 0.5 \\, \\text{m}\\), so the area \\(A = s^2 = (0.5)^2 = 0.25 \\, \\text{m}^2\\).\n- Initial magnetic field \\(B_i = 0.4 \\, \\text{T}\\).\n- Final magnetic field \\(B_f = 0\\).\n- Time interval \\(\\Delta t = 2 \\, \\text{s}\\).\n\n**Calculate the change in flux:**\n\n\\[\n\\Delta \\Phi_B = \\Phi_B \\text{(initial)} - \\Phi_B \\text{(final)} = (B_i \\cdot A) - (B_f \\cdot A) = (0.4 \\times 0.25) - (0 \\times 0.25) = 0.1 \\, \\text{Wb}\n\\]\n\nSince the magnetic field decreases uniformly to zero,\n\\[\n\\text{emf} = -\\frac{\\Delta \\Phi_B}{\\Delta t} = -\\frac{0.1}{2} = -0.05 \\, \\text{V}\n\\]\n\n**Ohm's Law:**  \nThe induced current \\(I\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\nI = \\frac{\\text{emf}}{R}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(R = 10^{-2} \\, \\Omega\\).\n\n\\[\nI = \\frac{-0.05}{10^{-2}} = -5 \\, \\text{A}\n\\]\n\nThe negative sign implies the direction is opposite to the assumed positive direction (counterclockwise by convention for decreasing field outwards). Thus, the direction of the induced current is clockwise.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nANSWER: C"
"To solve this, we need to balance the nuclear reaction in terms of mass number and atomic number.\n\nThe given reaction is:\n\\[ ^6_3\\text{Li} + ? \\rightarrow ^7_3\\text{Li} \\]\n\n1. **Balance Atomic Number (Bottom Number):**\n   - On the left side, we have atomic number 3 (from Li).\n   - On the right side, the atomic number remains 3 (from Li).\n   - Since the atomic number is 3 on both sides, the atomic number of the particle must be 0.\n\n2. **Balance Mass Number (Top Number):**\n   - On the left side, we have mass number 6 (from Li).\n   - On the right side, the mass number is 7 (from Li).\n   - This means the mass number of the particle must be 1 (since 6 + 1 = 7).\n\nGiven the options:\n- A. Alpha particle (\\(^4_2\\text{He}\\)): mass number 4, atomic number 2\n- B. Electron (\\(^0_{-1}e\\)): mass number 0, atomic number -1\n- C. Neutron (\\(^1_0n\\)): mass number 1, atomic number 0\n- D. Proton (\\(^1_1H\\)): mass number 1, atomic number 1\n\nOnly option C, a neutron, with mass number 1 and atomic number 0, fits the criteria.\n\nANSWER: C"
"To solve the problem, we use lens formula and magnification concepts for a converging lens.\n\n1. **Lens Formula**: \n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{u}\n   \\]\n   where \\(f\\) is the focal length, \\(v\\) is the image distance, and \\(u\\) is the object distance (taken as negative in lens formula).\n\n2. Here, the object distance \\(u = -1.5f\\).\n\n3. Plugging into the lens formula:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{v} + \\frac{1}{1.5f}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{1}{f} - \\frac{2}{3f} = \\frac{3}{3f} - \\frac{2}{3f} = \\frac{1}{3f}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, \\(v = 3f\\).\n\n4. Since \\(v\\) is positive, the image is real and located on the opposite side of the lens from the object.\n\n5. **Magnification (m)**:\n   \\[\n   m = \\frac{v}{u} = \\frac{3f}{-1.5f} = -2\n   \\]\n   The magnification of \\(-2\\) indicates that the image is inverted (real) and twice the size of the object.\n\nBased on the options:\n- **Real** and **Larger** fits perfectly.\n\nThus, the correct answer is:\nANSWER: D"
"To determine when the duck stops its forward motion, we need to find the point where the velocity is zero on the graph. \n\nObserving the graph:\n- At point A, the velocity is greater than zero.\n- At point B, the velocity is positive.\n- At point C, the velocity is still positive, though decreasing.\n- At point D, the graph reaches the x-axis, indicating that the velocity is zero.\n\nTherefore, the duck stops its forward motion at point D.\n\nANSWER: D."
"To determine between which two points the voltmeter would read zero, we need to identify two points that are at the same electrical potential.\n\nIn the given circuit, let's analyze the paths and nodes:\n\n1. The resistors form two parallel paths:\n   - Upper path: \\( \\text{T} \\rightarrow \\text{W} \\rightarrow \\text{U} \\rightarrow \\text{P} \\)\n   - Lower path: \\( \\text{T} \\rightarrow \\text{S} \\rightarrow \\text{Q} \\rightarrow \\text{P} \\)\n\n2. Both paths connect the same battery terminals and have equal resistance (since all resistors are identical). This makes them equivalent voltage dividers.\n\n3. Since both paths have the same resistance distribution and connect to the same points, each pair of points vertically aligned across these paths is at the same potential.\n\n4. Now looking at the points:\n   - \\( \\text{W} \\) corresponds with \\( \\text{S} \\)\n   - \\( \\text{U} \\) corresponds with \\( \\text{Q} \\)\n\nTherefore, the voltmeter will read zero when it is connected between two points with the same potential. Thus, the correct answer corresponds to points \\( \\text{Q} \\) and \\( \\text{U} \\).\n\nANSWER: A"
